# A name bound to what the language's own code computes of names, with no call, stands for those names (#328), and
# the body's own code reads those names where it reads the name, never where it binds it (#343): `named` binds the gap
# between the level and the module's quiet level (`first.db - QUIET.db`) and the list of both (`[first, QUIET]`) to names
# before it hands each to a step, and `written` writes each where its step is handed it. An operator is no occurrence and
# a name adds no arrow, so the two routes are one plan: each step takes the level and the quiet level, which alone comes
# from beyond, and the route's own arrow reads neither where the body binds a name to them.

route signal.named
step named/_draw
step named/_show

line entry > named call
line Level·first > named value
line Level·first > named/_draw value                 # `_draw(gap)`, `gap = first.db - QUIET.db`
line Level·QUIET > named/_draw value                 # the module's quiet level, from beyond
line Level·first > named/_show value                 # `_show(both)`, `both = [first, QUIET]`
line Level·QUIET > named/_show value
line named/_draw > Screen own
line named/_show > Screen own

home Level·first named
home Level·QUIET named

side entry left
side Screen right

route signal.written
step written/_draw
step written/_show

line entry > written call
line Level·first > written value
line Level·first > written/_draw value               # `_draw(first.db - QUIET.db)`
line Level·QUIET > written/_draw value
line Level·first > written/_show value               # `_show([first, QUIET])`
line Level·QUIET > written/_show value
line written/_draw > Screen own
line written/_show > Screen own

home Level·first written
home Level·QUIET written

side entry left
side Screen right

# A name a step changes in place holds a value of its own from its binding on, and stands for nothing (#355): `gathered`
# binds a list of the first level (`[first]`) and appends the second to it. The body's own code makes the list where it
# binds it, and reads the first level there; the append is handed that list and the second level and makes the list
# anew, and the step that shows it takes what the append made. No step takes the first level.

route signal.gathered
step gathered/append
step gathered/_show

line entry > gathered call
line Level·first > gathered value                    # `both: list[Level] = [first]`
line Level·second > gathered value
line Level·both@gathered/~both > gathered/append value # `both.append(second)`
line Level·second > gathered/append value
line gathered/append > Level·both@gathered/append value
line Level·both@gathered/append > gathered/_show value # `_show(both)`
line gathered/_show > Screen own

home Level·first gathered
home Level·second gathered
home Level·both@gathered/~both gathered
home Level·both@gathered/append gathered

side entry left
side Screen right

# A choice's value is what its arms make of it (#366): `chosen` binds a list of the first level and changes it in one
# arm. The change takes the list the body made where it binds the name, which stands in the route's frame and not in
# the arm's, and makes it anew; the arm that does not run keeps it, so what the change makes is one value at the choice's
# moment, and the step after the choice takes that value, as it would of a list a step made.

route signal.chosen
choice chosen/?1                                       # `if loud`
step chosen/?1/append
step chosen/_show

line entry > chosen call
line Level·first > chosen value                        # `both: list[Level] = [first]`, read where it is bound
line Level·second > chosen value
line Level·both@chosen/~both > chosen/?1/append value  # `both.append(second)`
line Level·second > chosen/?1/append value
line chosen/?1/append > Level·both@chosen/?1 value     # made anew in one arm, kept in the other
line Level·both@chosen/?1 > chosen/_show value         # `_show(both)`
line chosen/_show > Screen own

home Level·first chosen
home Level·second chosen
home Level·both@chosen/~both chosen
home Level·both@chosen/?1 chosen

side entry left
side Screen right
